In article <1993Feb12.191641.12837@mksol.dseg.ti.com> mccall@mksol.dseg.ti.com (fred j mccall 575-3539) writes:

> >theft is punished because the damage that is done by theft is the
> >deprivation of the owner of his legal possession... or what?
> >Maybe this position has to be re-worked, but I think this shows that
> >it is questionable to equal intellectual with material property...

> Not at all.  You are depriving him of part of the value of his work in
> developing the intellectual property in the first place.  If you can
> just take it, why should people pay for it? 

One of the reasons why I support the idea of free software. And don't
say, "free software works in academia but not in business" - you can
hire programmers and pay them for writing free software, and make your
money with selling hardware or support - both of which are not
vulnerable to piracy because of the nature of the matter...

> If people don't have to
> pay for it before they use it, why should anyone *do* it?

This is the reason why most software may not even be test-run before
purchase. Imagine buying a car without a test ride...

> Essentially, theft of intellectual property is theft of income.  While
> someone might not use something if they had to pay for it, that's no
> good excuse for simply stealing it.

I don't regard my arguments as an excuse for stealing. As long as a
programmer regards his work as property in the conventional sense, I
think is is morally wrong to copy it without paying, since it *can*
(not inevitably does, however) deprive him of income. But it is
problematic for the property-holder to justify this property argument
with simply saying, "This is mine."

> This is like saying that shoplifting isn't theft because the shop
> still has all those *other* things to sell.

At least this comparison seems wrong to me, as software copies can be
produced in *arbitrary* numbers at *no* cost. If I take a book out of
10 from a shop, the shop can sell only the remaining 9. If a shop had
10 copies of a software product and I did copy one of them, they could
still sell all 10 of them. Seen in this light, the shop doesn't get
deprived of one copy. I can even take the one copied item and make 100
of it, and the shop can *still* sell all 10 of them.

The problem is finite vs. potentially infinite resources, and people
trying to regard those definitely different things as equal.

Olaf
