FTP password files are a valid target of crackers.  I've been shown 
shell scripts that root out anonymous FTP sites (by walking through
the DNS tables) and copy their password file *just in case* the site
admin was stupid enough put real data in them.  When a colleague of
mine opened a site for anonymous ftp, the ~ftp/etc/passwd file was
retrieved over 30 times in the first 3 weeks.

If you suggest that these 30+ people were all naive users who mistakenly
downloaded ~ftp/etc/passwd (and, in most cases, ONLY ~ftp/etc/passwd), 
I'll have to laugh.

Anonymous FTP password files *are* searched by crackers.  If possible,
I think that most of us would like to stop crackers before they cause 
damage.  Therefore, some sites attempt to trace such actions back to 
the source.  This is what SRI did, no more and no less.  If the admins 
at Memphis State chose to go haywire and involve everyone they could, 
that isn't anyone's problem but MSU's.

