In article <1lof26$cig@nz12.rz.uni-karlsruhe.de> s_titz@ira.uka.de (Olaf
Titz) writes:

>One of the reasons why I support the idea of free software. And don't
>say, "free software works in academia but not in business" - you can
>hire programmers and pay them for writing free software, and make your
>money with selling hardware or support - both of which are not
>vulnerable to piracy because of the nature of the matter...

This removes one of the major powers of the market system--high rewards for
popular (in demand) products, and low rewards, or even penalties for poor or
low demand stuff. Paying someone to write software and then giving it out is
not that different from the centrally determined non-market principles that
destroyed the former Soviet Union.

>
>This is the reason why most software may not even be test-run before
>purchase. Imagine buying a car without a test ride...

It is up to the property owner whether he wishes to permit test rides or
not. Imagine taking a car off the lot without permission because you wanted
a test ride. You'd end up in jail very quickly. It isn't up to you or me to
"take" software without permission and justify it with the test ride
argument.

>
>At least this comparison seems wrong to me, as software copies can be
>produced in *arbitrary* numbers at *no* cost. If I take a book out of
>10 from a shop, the shop can sell only the remaining 9. If a shop had
>10 copies of a software product and I did copy one of them, they could
>still sell all 10 of them. Seen in this light, the shop doesn't get
>deprived of one copy. I can even take the one copied item and make 100
>of it, and the shop can *still* sell all 10 of them.

It has been many years since the notion of property was limited to
non-duplicatable physical entities. This above argument flies in the face of
everything we have learned about intellectual property, stimulation of R&D,
etc. Intellectual property, though governed by unique laws, is just as
valuable as physical property. To see this just look at the audited,
certified books of any producer of intellectual property to see the value
imputed to it by generally accepted accounting principles.

There are entire companies whose market value is based on the intangible
intellectual property they "own" and have the sole right to exploit. You may
not like this, but that isn't the same as arguing that there's no value just
because the number of physical copies the owner has is unchanged.

Finally, it is not cost, but value that counts on the demand side. It's none
of the buyer's business what something costs to make--just what the
purchasers who do buy are willing to pay.

