In article <1993Feb15.155508.27308@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:
>"~ftp/etc/passwd" is not their "site's passwd file". I concede that
>"access to our site's passwd file" is an "activity that is very often
>associated with hackers." I maintain, however, that the student did
>not attempt to access their "site's passwd file" so it is unfair
>associate the student's activity with "activity that is very often
>associated with hackers."

The anonymous FTP user did "get /etc/passwd".  Since /etc/passwd is the
name of the Unix password file, I think this could reasonable be
interpreted as attempting to get the site's password file.  Luckily,
anonymous FTP normally runs chroot'ed so the system interprets this as
~ftp/etc/passwd, and the AI.SRI.COM administrators are smart enough not to
put a copy of /etc/passwd there, so he didn't actually access anything
critical.  But the issue is what he possibly *attempted* or *hoped* to do.

Others have taken me to task for saying that he "was *clearly* trying to
access their password file".  Barry Shein, whose opinion I usually respect,
proposed an incredible scenario where a completely innocent, Unix-naive
person was just downloading a file that a friend mentioned the name of, or
because he was trying to figure out what the cryptic name "passwd" means,
or something like that.  I think these scenarios are possible, but
unlikely; Unix is hardly an obscure system, and you'd have to be pretty
dense not to be able to guess what's in a file named "passwd".  It's much
more likely that the user has a little Unix knowledge, but doesn't know
that anonymous FTP has a private, usually useless, copy of /etc/passwd.

But I'm willing to admit when I'm wrong: it's not crystal clear that he's
trying to break into your system.  But it's certainly enough reason to be
wary.  To make a legal analogy, it's not enough evidence to convict, but it
should be enough probable cause for an investigation.

