>>>the source.  This is what SRI did, no more and no less.  If the admins 
>>>at Memphis State chose to go haywire and involve everyone they could, 
>>>that isn't anyone's problem but MSU's.

The concept of the net's "cooperative nature" has been held in high
regard by many of the participants in this discussion; in fact, one
commentator mentioned that anonymous FTP was a shining example of this
"cooperative spirit."  Why is cooperation among admins in situations
such as this immediately trampled as admin fascism and Big Brotherhood?  

If the admin at foo.bar.edu discovered that one of his users was (pos-
sibly) forging email/postings in your name, you'd be *really* pleased 
if he let you know about it, even if your only action was to review
your email/news logfiles; if the admin at baz.snooker.com let you know 
that one of your users was (possibly) trying to attack his systems' 
sendmail daemon, you'd probably help his investigation.

Let's not assume that the 'cooperative spirit' of the net extends only
to its users; those who manage it can (and should) cooperate as well.
There is certainly a potential for overreaction (the SRI-MSU incident
is a perfect example), but we cannot fault the entire concept with
one incident; if that were the case, we could probably name several anony-
mous FTP sites that taint all others by distributing illegal materials
(or by distributing legitimate materials illegally).

