In article <1993Feb15.194649.7894@netcom.com> strnlght@netcom.com (David Sternlight) writes:
 
[Free software]
> This removes one of the major powers of the market system--high rewards for
> popular (in demand) products, and low rewards, or even penalties for poor or
> low demand stuff. Paying someone to write software and then giving it out is
> not that different from the centrally determined non-market principles that
> destroyed the former Soviet Union.

It is very different. Since software is not a physical entity, the
traditional market economy does not work for software, which I wanted
to explain when stating that software piracy is different from the
traditional notion of theft. See below on a working example in a
market economy.

> It is up to the property owner whether he wishes to permit test rides or
> not. Imagine taking a car off the lot without permission because you wanted
> a test ride. You'd end up in jail very quickly. It isn't up to you or me to
> "take" software without permission and justify it with the test ride
> argument.

I agree in this, but note that in the auto business, someone who
doesn't permit test rides isn't going to sell too many cars. This is
the force of the market.

> It has been many years since the notion of property was limited to
> non-duplicatable physical entities. This above argument flies in the face of
> everything we have learned about intellectual property, stimulation of R&D,
> etc. Intellectual property, though governed by unique laws, is just as
> valuable as physical property. To see this just look at the audited,

It certainly is as valuable, but since it is a different thing, we
should IMHO devise new laws that fit the unique situation better
rather than applying the traditional laws of physical entities.

> not like this, but that isn't the same as arguing that there's no value just
> because the number of physical copies the owner has is unchanged.

Not *no value*. There is certainly such a thing as intellectual value.
The question is, how can this value be brought to the market?

In Germany (I don't know about how this is handled in the U.S.) the
advent of cheap audio cassette recorders at end-60s brought up the
question of how to protect the intellectual property of music
performers from pirates, and first the lawmakers decided to make it
simply illegal to record a broadcast performance from the radio or
copy a record. But soon it was realized that this is unenforcable, so
they found a better solution: There already exists an agency which
collects royalties for musicians (GEMA) and now from every cassette
and every cassette recorder sold a certain amount of money is
collected by GEMA.  This is not exactly pure free market economy but
far away from the Soviet Union, IMHO. A similar solution is now into
effect for photocopiers. The software business has a lot to learn from
this example.

> Finally, it is not cost, but value that counts on the demand side. It's none
> of the buyer's business what something costs to make--just what the
> purchasers who do buy are willing to pay.

Exactly, and this is precisely the reason why pirating of software is
so common - people are not willing to pay the price software producers
demand. Since there *is* a difference between theft of physical
entities and software pirating and many people have no high regard for
the value of software, they do pirate.

I'm in no way to justify this. But these simple facts have to be
considered when thinking about a better solution for the software
business and against the current situation, which satisfies nobody
except the dedicated pirates.

