I only partly blame SRI. I don't think it is unreasonable for a
company to notify another site when the passwd file is snarfed, even a
"dummy" passwd file. We send a similar notice ourself (I think the
notice in question is a "canned" one that comes with one of the
security packages.)
  I *do* think the university over-reacted, but I also think the
STUDENT over-reacted. If he was asked to explain himself, and he did,
and no further action was taken, well, I'm sorry that he had a scare,
but he accidentally comitted an action that looked awfully like what a
hacker might have done, so I don't see the big problem with asking his
system administrator to ask him what was up.
  Getting the dean involved was overkill, I think. Getting the net
involved was overkill, also.
  I've debated here before that I don't think talking to people is
necessarily the same as threatening them. 
  I disagree that the student should have been contacted first. It is
reasonable and proper for two organizations to contact each other
through a "chain of command." If I actually had a cracker on my system
I would not want him or her warned off by a victim. In this situation,
where an innocent student **accidentally mimicked a cracker** (In My
Humble Interpretation)  I think the site admin did the right thing.
  There are two very separate issues here: the issue of taking a
site's passwd file or fake passwd file, and the issue of taking files
from a site which does not want their anonymous ftp server used by the
public. We all seem to be in agreement on the second point.

