To assume that "passwd" files are necessarily hacker-related is 
equally disingenuous.
 
In Practical Unix Security, Gene Spafford writes:
 
     UNIX uses the /etc passwd file to keep track of every user on the
     system. /etc/passwd contains the username, real name, identification
     information, and basic account information for each user...
    
     You an use cat(1) to display your system's /etc/passwd. Here are a
     few sample lines from a typical file: (p 23)
     
This, in context of Spaf's section on the /etc/passwd file suggests
that there is nothing remiss in accessing such files. Those of use
who learn UNIX through his and similar books routinely experiment with
the commands provided. This may include downloading such files for 
experimentation by following the commands provided. In the
absence of proscriptions, and in the presence of presumed acceptable
norms, then assuming accessibility strikes me is legitimate.
 
Spaf also adds (p. 39) that:
 
    If you system does not have shadow passwords, then you should
    take extra precautions to ensure that the /etc/passwd file cannot 
    be read anonymously, either over the network or with the UUCP system.     
     
Make of it what you will.
 
