The discussion was about whether one had the right to take the law into
one's one hands by pirating a copy of software and then defending that
action with the "try before you buy" argument.

The point was that one could not take a car off the lot without permission
to test drive it without perhaps ending up in jail.

Thus the phrase "up to him" and "that's his business" refer to who gets the
legal right to decide whether to permit test drives by potential
customers--the dealer or owner of a car, or some customer off the street.
These points are being used by analogy with whether someone has the right to
use the try before you buy argument to infringe a software copyright or
patent.

By saying that "the policy is of relevance" to a potential car buyer, Thomas
is twisting words to create yet another red herring (a highly visible
response that is actually irrelevant to the point and is intended to shift
the discussion in order to avoid that point).

It is exactly such behavior that convinces me that there's little use in
continuing this particular sub-dialogue.

