Bill Hughes writes:
 
    >Jim, has anyone ever determined if (at least some of) the
    >reduction in software piracy is due to lower retail costs of the
    >software and/or favorable discounts to educational
    >institutions/large sites/favored groups? Here at UTSA, I am able
    >to request many software packages at as much as sixty percent off
    >manufacturer's suggested retail. Note that this is _not_ via
    >state contract, site license, or any other such deal --these are
    >discounts direct from the publisher simply because my research
    >center is associated with a University. I also get some personal
    >fallout --I've been offered software for my perrsonal use at deep
    >discounts, again because I am associated with UTSA.
 
Bill, that's a tough question. There has been little systematic
empirical research of which I'm aware on the explicit question.
Inferences from limited data sources can give us a suggestive picture.
 
Your implied assumption is probably right.  Focusing on the Northern
Illinois corridor, which includes Chicago, the suburbs, Rockford (and
N. Ill. U, where I'm at), piracy has decreased for a variety of
reasons.
 
1) At NIU, presumably like other schools, we are aggressively hit with
ad campaigns for discounts that can run as high as 80-90 pct for some
major products. Judging from colleagues, the campaigns have been
highly successful.
 
2) For university personnel, "piracy" has been dramatically lowered by
site license availability. Between '89-91 we did a qualitative study
that included some of these issues. Then, among the most highly
"borrowed" programs included Word Perfect, dBase, Harvard Graphics,
and SPSS-PC. The university created a policy that allowed all of these
(and many other) programs to be made available to faculty and others
by purchasing a site license.  The response, especially from
individual faculty, was so overwheming that a separate fulltime
position was created to manage the paperwork. I could dig up the exact
figures if you want them, but if memory serves, currently over 1,200
faculty purchased a Word Perfect license alone. The upgrades are
timely for those who want them, and technical support is more than
adequate.  This effectively undercut the motivation to "pirate." I'd
wager that it would be very difficult to find illicit copies of these
programs among University folk.  To reverse a common adage, why should
I pirate a $400 program if I can obtain the full set of disks for
1/20th of the cost?
 
3) The drop of software prices and the increased competitiveness of
"street" dealers also has had an impact.  It's simply more affordable.
 
4) Package deals are also popular. "By 1, get 4." Both companies and
dealers have utilized this strategy. Presumably, the hook at that once
you buy it, you'll invest in inexpensive upgrades, which creates a
semi-permanent market.
 
5) Perhaps I'm wrong (and hope somebody correct me if I am), but PC
purchases today include systems loaded with licit software.  If I
receive the programs I need upon purchase, where's the motivation to
"pirate?"
 
Market forces have, it seems, played a significant role in reducing
piracy.  Advertising, competitive pricing, gimmicks, and creative
availability simply make it easier to purchase than pirate.
 
In addition to economic factors, social cultural factors also should
be noted: 1) Education of ethical and legal software use; 2) Changes
in the "pirate culture," which has changed dramatically since the
mids-1980s; 3) Increased utility of programs, so one can purchase a
single program where several might have been necessary before; 4) A
decrease in the mystique and novelty of software, which means that
it's so pleniful (licitly) that it's a waste of time and energy to
find, obtain, and use an illicit copy.
 
Note that the above applies to "adult/business" programs, not
"kidz/gamerz." I have no idea what kids are doing nowadays. Our study
of a few years ago focused primarily on adults/business.
 
The biggest economic drain is coming from overseas bootlegging, which,
according to the Business Software Alliance, results in losses of at
least $10-12 billion a year, mostly to larger companies such as Aldus,
Borland, and the rest. Even if that figure is grossly inflated, it's
still a substantial loss.
 
So, a combination of market forces and social factors seem far
more effective in reducing the "piracy problem" than some of the
suggstions "zero-tolerance" advocates propose.
I also caution the my comments refer to casual domestic end-users,
and not to bootleggers or foreign problems.
 
Jim Thomas
 

