In article <1993Feb19.055054.13283@netcom.com> strnlght@netcom.com (David Sternlight) writes:

>Has nobody thought that one reason piracy may be down is that it is no
>longer a "fuzzy" matter of copyright infringement where you get warned, etc.
>but now a felony where you get to go to jail?
 
You seem not to have paid attention. PL 102-561 (the so-called anti-piracy
law, even though it does not mention software) was passed quickly and signed
October ('92). Piracy has been in decline since 1989. Generally, and this
case seems no different, causal primacy requires that a cause precede the
effect. Further, the new law requires more than simply "copyright
infringement." It specifies precisely, although too narrowly, a specific
range of reproduction and distribution acts. The SPA's visibility wasn't
wide-spread until 1992 (even though their first suit was in March '88).
I'd wager that few sysops of "pirate" boards, most of whom are kids,
know what an "SPA" is, let alone that there's a federal law in existence.
 
>I'll bet that once the word got around about the new felony law, a lot of
>"elite" bulletin boards suddenly changed their spots.
 
Empirically false. For reasons unrelated to deterrence, "elite" pirate
boards were in decline by the end of '90. I can think of no so-called
Top Ten board that existed at the end of '91. Much of this had to do 
with the proliferation of cheap high speed modems, the ease of creating
a BBS, and the increase of modemers. Neighborhood/local BBSes 
increased dramatically. Few BBSes last more than 18 months, most much less.
The demise of the "elite pirate board" was also affected by the 
proliferation of software: It was simply too time consuming to 
run a good board. The availabity of software as discussed in previous
posts also undercut the motivation. There are numerous boards around that
include among their files copyright software, but the bulk are run by
young folk of college or highschool age. Especially those specializing in
games. Although I'm sure there will always be "pirate" boards, they
are currently a cultural anachronism. I doubt that they'll be a significant
factor within two years, and the cause isn't criminalization.
 
It should be noted that even though PL 102-561 does not mention software,
it seems clear from the legislative history that software was an explicit
concern. Was EFF responsible for altering some of this wording?
 
Jim Thomas 


