In article <1993Feb19.060315.14305@netcom.com> strnlght@netcom.com (David Sternlight) writes:
>As a separate point, I mention that emacs is quirky, obscure, and notorious
>for being hard to learn. It is, in fact, a nerd's editor and not one for the
>general computer user. (I say this as someone who went to the same school
>and studied computers at the same place as Stallman, MIT.) I, too, was a boy
>genius. But I know the difference between a product for me and my friends,
>and one for the general user. I point out that that's what happens when
>someone develops a "free" program that doesn't have to meet a market test. I
>assert that had emacs been sold head-to-head with other products whose
>developers knew they had to satisfy the customer, not themselves, either it
>would have had a dramatically different interface or it would have gone
>belly up.

First of all, as someone else pointed out, Emacs doesn't compete
head-to-head with products like Word, because they serve different purposes
and are targeted to different audiences.  Emacs is a great editor for
programmers, while Word is great for word processing.  Does Word have any
facilities for automatic indentation of programming constructs?

Does anyone know whether all the commercial Emacs-style editors have gone
belly up?  On Unix there have been at least two: Unipress Emacs and CCA
Emacs.  On the PC there was Epsilon (during the short time I was doing PC
programming it was the only thing that made the process bearable -- it even
had shell buffers and background compiling).  If these products are still
around, then that definitely refutes your claim.  They're just as much
"nerd's editors" as GNU Emacs, but they managed to succeed commercially.

In the case of Unipress and CCA Emacs, their failure wouldn't indicate a
failure of this style of editor, though.  They may have lost their market
to GNU Emacs (we used to buy CCA Emacs, but switched years ago to GNU), not
to the fancy word processors.  At our site, 95% of the Unix editing is done
using GNU Emacs, and fancy word processors like Interleaf and Frame are
used only by a handful of documentation people (at $5-10K/user, we can't
afford to have everyone using these systems).

And in the case of PC's, the biggest competition against Epsilon is not
likely to be Word, but the editors that are often packaged with compilers
to form integrated programming environments.  It's hard to compete against
bundled products.

Finally, one of the ideas of the free software developers is that they can
support a commercial service market: contract customization and support.
Companies like Cygnus Support show that this process works.
