>At least this comparison seems wrong to me, as software copies can be
>produced in *arbitrary* numbers at *no* cost. If I take a book out of
>10 from a shop, the shop can sell only the remaining 9. If a shop had
>10 copies of a software product and I did copy one of them, they could
>still sell all 10 of them. Seen in this light, the shop doesn't get
>deprived of one copy. I can even take the one copied item and make 100
>of it, and the shop can *still* sell all 10 of them.
	The interesting thing about this whole issue is that software
	is not the only thing that can be copied this way.  When books
	get distributed "on-line" the same techology will result
	in books being "pirated" in the same manner as software.
	What's more, there will be no way to stop it.  Currently
	book publishers are reluctant to put books in "on-line" 
	form because they *know* what the result will be. As a matter of
	fact, scanner technology and associated software will make
	books available "on-line" whether or not publishers cooperate.
	
	What we saw happening with the copyright law with respect to
	both xerography and the taping of television programming was a 
	recognition of a very important concept:  IF VIOLATING THE LAW 
	WITH IMPUNITY BECOMES VERY EASY, IT IS THE LAW THAT MUST CHANGE.  
	UNENFORCEABLE LAWS UNDERMINE THE ENTIRE LEGAL SYSTEM.  

	The SPA antics of lately are just the dying gasps of the old system 
	of accounting for software and information in general.  Although it 
	is difficult for some of us to see it today, the future outlook on 
	the economic nature of software will be closer to Stallman's view 
	than to the SPA's view.  

	The problem is this: given that "intellectual property" cannot
	be made to work in the technological context of the near future
	(some might say it has already broken down) how can we set up
	structures that will foster the development of innovative 
	software solutions without relying on the unworkable concept
	of "intellectual property".  My problem with our current society's
	fixation on protecting "intellectual property" is that it
	only postpones the inevitable and does not favor the development
	of new social and economic structures that will replace the
	broken system.  This is short-sighted and unproductive in the
	long run.  

>The problem is finite vs. potentially infinite resources, and people
>trying to regard those definitely different things as equal.
	Exactly!  And that is why information (not just software but
	info of any type) is NOT REALLY AN ECONOMIC QUANTITY.  It is
	this observation that makes so called "intellectual property"
	a totally bankrupt concept.  You cannot sell things that are
	not an economic quantity.  If you attempt to impose artificial
	constraints on the supply to make them appear economic the
	mechanism for doing it will become corrupt and foster disregard
	for the entire system of laws that govern the society.




