Actually, I ran academic systems for many years and often used that
line.

Given that, and the content of the note this was contained in, I'll
make a comment about academic system administration vs commercial:

1. Academia is by and large run like a degenerate socialist state,
there is nearly no relationship between the "customer" and his or her
desires and how one sets their priorities. That this does not seem to
make sense has almost no bearing on the matter. Few universities, for
example, have anything connecting student recruiting or any measure of
their satisfaction and administrative resource policies. Somehow the
universities muddle by.

2. Priorities and policies are set almost exclusively by internal
bickering and politics having almost nothing to do with what most
would call rationality.

As an extreme but not entirely wild example I once had a nobel prize
winner who everyone worshipped come into the dept after being away
over a year and on his way back out for another year and insist on
reviewing all the current purchasing etc plans for computing, look
them over, restructure them radically, and fly out of the country, he
never even used these systems, ever (hell, I don't think he ever even
took his coat off, no kidding.)  And ya know what, everyone acted like
they *had* to therefore do what he said and proceeded to spend $150K
on some really questionable equipment. Weird is an understatement.

3. Academic systems are mostly run by people who are not academics.
That doesn't say anything either way, but don't think what you know
about professors tells you anything about academic systems, it
doesn't, profs are usually victims of the campus computing policies as
much as students, oftentimes more so (or at the very least don't get
to leave after 4 years.)

4. Unfortunately academic computing centers attract some of the more
bizarre people I've ever met. Many of those who succeed in management
at such centers seem to have a deep-seated hatred of all things
academic, students, faculty, etc. Some are petty sadists (I am not
kidding nor exagerrating) who get their kicks out of making a student
squirm, I have no idea why, maybe they didn't get into some fraternity
they wanted when they were undergrads. I could name names...

5. One of the fastest ways to isolate yourself in these orgs is to let
it be known your concerns are on behalf of students or faculty,
especially students, rather than just paying attention to the local
pecking order. I was generally considered suspicious merely because I
taught academic courses. As far as I could tell this was considered to
be a conflict of loyalties. The two people directly above me (going up
the totem pole) prided themselves in the fact that they had *never*
(or only once) taught a course in their lives and never would.

Anyhow, being the big rooster strutting around some academic computing
center may seem like something to an undergrad, but in this big wide
world it, well, it doesn't always attract the most well constructed
people.

