First, while in academia I often supported the idea of turning over
non-technical administration and management of campus computing to the
libraries. It was always met with derision but I still think it's a
great idea, these are people who have spent a lot of time figuring out
how to give away things to a public in an orderly and fair fashion, as
well as documenting and cataloguing everything (including policies.)

It's unlikely to happen in our lifetime because of the political
structure of most universities which puts libraries way at the bottom
of the heap (sad but true) and things like computing near the top (for
those who administer them.) Hard to explain, but the reaction would be
"it's just not done." That is, you don't give good things to the
librarians. Again, sad but true.

But overall I'd rather take my chances with the librarians figuring
out how to manage the technical side of things (including their
ability to just hire staff to do that, they don't have to do it
themselves of course) than take my chances with your typical academic
computing center administrator figuring out how to manage a campus
resource and ever recognize that there are people behind those account
names.

I think you'd find that admins in commercial sites are much more
liberal about policies than anything that would be tolerated in
academia (other than hard lines about information leakage.) One reason
is because the people using their systems actually have jobs to do and
can't be crippled with stupid policies that just raise the comfort
level. You can do that to students (without end it would seem), but
not co-workers (obviously exceptions exist), not if you expect the
company to be profitable.

When I moved from academia to a more industrial site I was amazed how
open things were, almost anyone who could possibly need it had the
root password to their large servers (and few problems occurred), disk
space was bought (typically depts bought their own disks to plug into
the server) so wasn't really fought over except w/in a dept perhaps.
It all tended to be relatively rational and easy to accept (you want
disk space? get some dept'l budget and buy some.)

Something more abstract was just that people took each other seriously
and assumed that the other guy having a problem or whatever was driven
by something the company needed and hence might be related to your
(everyone's) paycheck.

It wasn't heaven for other reasons, but most of this kind of stuff was
just run rationally and based on needs rather than weird policies from
even weirder committees or mandarin administrators.

Most academic computing administrators wouldn't survive very long in a
commercial site, they're usually too obsessed with control first and
getting work done as only a by-product when everything else is
satisfied. Companies which are out to make money have very short fuses
about attitudes like that, and not just in computing.

