>goes.  You contend that there would not be any software as 
>*sophisticated* as Microsoft Word if free software was the norm.
>[And I wish you'd quit confusing market share with sophistication.]

I don't confuse the two. I am not making a market share argument at all, but
an argument that lots of people are willing to pay for something. Why?
Because the pay product contains features and ease of use they believe they
can't get free. I don't consider sophistication to mean hard-to-learn niche
features or a confusing interface.

In those terms, people pay a lot for software that, on inspection, has not
been developed by the free software market. I argue that if the "free" crowd
could have developed it, they would. Since they have not, I argue that it
takes a financial incentive to develop, program, test, market and support
the pay software that the free software context lacks.

The only possible counter-argument, which a)has not been made, and b) is
completely speculative and rests only on assertion, is:
If all software were free the folks who developed the pay stuff would have
developed equal or better stuff free.

Any other arguments must fail, since they are refuted by the empirical
evidence of a vast pay software world selling products that people actually
buy, in huge numbers. If the free software world had produced products that
met the same buyers' needs equally, why would they pay for a pay product?
The answer must be that in the test of the market the pay software won
either because it was better, or because no free equivalent existed. In
either case the argument for making all software free fails, since one can
write free software without such a "law" and yet it has not been written to
meet the needs people are now paying for.

I have never argued, nor do I now, that free software shouldn't exist.
People are free to write what they like, and I'm grateful for those free
products which save me money. But to make the entire market free would lose
many people the valuable programs they've shown they're willing to pay for,
and if the free people really believe in freedom (and power to the people
isn't some fascist notion of power to me and my friends) then they must also
accept the right of others to write pay software, protect it, and charge
whatever the market will pay for it.

At bottom the "free software" argument is one by some people who want
something for nothing and are unwilling to pay. It is also just another
rationalization of the pirate. I say this because those on the supply side
who write and sell free software need not make any argument--they have
every right to produce and distribute free software, and are doing so now.
No agreement, law or change is needed for them to function.

Thus those who ARGUE for this want something changed. Either they want
another's work for nothing without the agreement of that other,  or (for
some few on the supply side mentioned above) they can't stand the heat of
better pay products, and would like to see the pay products put out of
business.

Penultimately, there are those who really think everything should be free.
They are not arguing software but some economic ideology. if that's what
they're up to they should say so, because the general argument is both
inappropriate for this newsgroup (in my view), and I think so few believe it
is an open question that I would not participate in such a discussion.

Finally, the writer congratulates himself for being civil by avoiding ad
hominem attacks. That's a sad comment on some participants here. It would
seem that the rules of civilized discourse don't apply in unmoderated
newsgroups, and (with rare exceptions) there is no social pressure to
substitute for a moderator in keeping a civil tongue in some heads.

I suggest that if the rational and civil people here don't criticize such ad
hominem and bullying tactics as soon as they occur, to cite Pastor
Niemoller's famous remark soon they will come for you--that is: many won't
participate and you'll have the "power to me and my friends" crowd and a
few newcomers left to talk to.

