>channels, since there are no marketing expenses, few if any packaging
>expenses--is a natural monopoly under the Sternlight definition.)

Free software is not a natural monopoly, because it has essentially constant
average costs with volume after some small volume, rather than sharply
declining average costs with volume. In this respect it is quite different
from networks, which have very high capital costs on the front end. It is
thus a good market for perfect competition, rather than monopoly AMONG THE
FREE SOFTWARE PROVIDERS.  Note that that says nothing about the pay software
providers.

Haven't we had enough mindless Sternlight-bashing from such as Davis. I've
said repeatedly that it is not the Sternlight definition of natural
monopoly, but that of the analytic economic textbooks. All my definitional
quotations were from Bill Bauhmol's "Economics" text, and reflect the
standard definition of "natural monopoly" in the economics field.

