In article <1993Feb21.191301.4326@netcom.com> strnlght@netcom.com (David Sternlight) writes:
>I argue that if the "free" crowd
>could have developed it, they would. Since they have not, I argue that it
>takes a financial incentive to develop, program, test, market and support
>the pay software that the free software context lacks.

The free software development community may simply be smaller than the
restricted software development community.  This is likely because most
software developers *assume* that restricting redistribution and not
distributing sources are the only reasonable way to distribute software
(like you do).  Restricted software was all there was for so long that many
people believe that it's obviously correct.  However, it is only recently
that free software has had enough distribution channels (the net, in
particular) that it has become a noticeable force in the industry.

[Notice my use of the word "restricted" rather than "pay".  In the context
of software like GNU Emacs, the word "free" comes from "freedom", not "free
cost".  The FSF charges hundreds of dollars for their free software, and
RMS even recently sent out an appeal for people to buy from the FSF rather
than someone else, since it needs the money.  Oops, is that more "proof"
that free software doesn't work?]

Another possibility is that restricted software's apparent "sophistication"
is a response to the existence of free software.  Users probably aren't
going to purchase as many copies of restricted software when there's
equivalent free software.  Notice for instance that hardly anyone sells
*just* a C compiler, they're all packaged with fancy, integrated
development environments, which GCC doesn't really provide (most of the
pieces are there when you combine GCC, GDB, GNU Emacs, and GNU Make, but
many people like the simplicity of using a prepackaged tool).
